? What is a typeface?
+ A visual design family for characters and symbols
- A fixed pixel dimension for every displayed image
- A numerical code identifying an audio sample
- A timing rule for synchronising animation frames
= Typeface describes character design. The lesson distinguishes it from a font of a particular style and size.

? What distinguishes a serif typeface from a sans serif typeface?
+ Small finishing strokes occur at its main stroke ends
- Every character is stored as a separate video frame
- It always uses only uppercase alphabetic characters
- Its text cannot contain hyperlinks or punctuation
= Serifs are the small finishing features; sans serif omits them.

? Which typography adjustment changes spacing between a particular letter pair?
+ Kerning
- Leading
- Sampling
- Encoding
= Kerning adjusts a specific pair. Tracking affects a run of characters, and leading affects lines.

? Which term describes an upward stroke in a lowercase letter such as h?
+ Ascender
- Descender
- Baseline
- Code point
= An ascender rises above the main lowercase body; a descender extends below the baseline.

? What does leading adjust?
+ The spacing between lines of text
- The code point assigned to a letter
- The number of bytes in a video frame
- The size of a photographic palette
= Leading is line spacing. It is different from tracking and kerning.

? How is a bitmap font primarily represented?
+ Stored pixel patterns for its glyphs
- Audio frequencies for its characters
- Only hyperlinks between documents
- A collection of animation timelines
= Bitmap fonts store pixel patterns and are tied to designed pixel sizes.

? What is rasterization of an outline font?
+ Converting its glyph outlines into display pixels
- Converting its text into spoken audio samples
- Replacing all character meanings with hyperlinks
- Removing all line spacing from a printed document
= A display needs discrete pixel coverage even when the font is stored as scalable outlines.

? How does anti-aliasing improve the appearance of rasterized text?
+ It blends edge intensities to reduce perceived jaggedness
- It increases the physical number of pixels on the screen
- It converts all lowercase letters to uppercase letters
- It guarantees that every missing font becomes available
= Intermediate edge intensities help the boundary look smoother. They do not remove the screen's finite resolution.

? What is plain text?
+ Character content without embedded presentation formatting
- Text that must always use a decorative outline typeface
- Text stored only as noneditable photographic pixels
- A document that must contain animation and sound tracks
= Plain text can use ASCII or Unicode encodings. Plainness describes the absence of embedded presentation formatting.

? Which statement correctly compares ASCII with Unicode?
+ Unicode covers many scripts that basic ASCII cannot represent
- ASCII includes all Tamil and Sinhala letters by definition
- Unicode selects the same visible typeface on every device
- ASCII and Unicode are names for the same font file format
= Unicode provides a much broader character repertoire; an appropriate encoding and font are still required.

? A required typeface is absent on the user's computer. What does font mapping specify?
+ A substitute font to use in its place
- A new screen resolution for the display
- An audio codec for reading every label
- A timing interval for animation playback
= Font mapping chooses a fallback/substitute. Test coverage and layout because the substitute can change appearance.

? How many inches equal 72 typographic points under the lesson's point definition?
# 1
= One point equals 1/72 inch, so 72 × 1/72 = 1 inch. Visible glyph height can still vary between typefaces.
